= Vāstu Puruṣa Maṇḍala: Comparative, Coordinate-Based Report from Mānasāra, Mānuṣyālaya Candrikā, Māyamata, and Bṛhat Saṃhitā


== Executive summary


The *81-pada (9×9) Vāstu Puruṣa Maṇḍala* (“Paramasāyika”) is described in multiple classical sources as a *grid procedure* (draw 10 lines each way to yield 81 squares) and as a *deity-allocation schema* in which (at minimum) *32 deities occupy the perimeter squares* and an additional set of *inner deities—including a central Brahmā region—occupy interior squares*. The *Bṛhat Saṃhitā*’s Vāstuvidyā chapter provides the most explicit *square-numbered placements* for both perimeter (32) and inner deities and also explains how these deities correspond to *the body-points of the Vāstupuruṣa*. citeturn47view4turn47view0turn48view0

There are *real source-variations* in (a) *which deity is placed at certain boundary positions* (e.g., the first eastern/perimeter position is *Śikhi/Agni* in *Bṛhat Saṃhitā* vs. *Īśa/Īśāna* in the Mānuṣyālaya Candrikā summary tradition), and (b) *the identity-set for the 12 inner deities* (the *Bṛhat Saṃhitā* list differs substantially from the Mānuṣyālaya Candrikā list). The Mānasāra tradition further records explicit disagreements across authorities (including the *Māyamata*) for several assignments. citeturn40view0turn15view2turn14view1

For *marma (vital) avoidance*, the Mānuṣyālaya Candrikā commentary tradition is unusually explicit: in the 81-pada layout it describes (i) *ten “nāḍī” lines to east and ten to north*, (ii) special *rajju (diagonal) lines*, and (iii) *100 marma-sthāna* generated by intersections of major sūtra-lines—explicitly declaring them *to be avoided for walls/columns* and giving a classical mitigation rule (a shift of ~1/24 of a pada from the marma-sūtra) plus a ritual remedy. citeturn39view0turn49view0

For *Āyādi Ṣaḍvarga*, the Mānuṣyālaya Candrikā commentary preserves a full *formula set* (expressed as multipliers and divisors on a house’s “paridhi”/perimeter or combined measures), including: *Yoni remainder (÷8), Vyaya remainder (÷14; and an alternate tradition), Āya remainder (÷12), Nakṣatra remainder (÷27), Tithi remainder (÷30), and Vāra remainder (÷7)*. It explicitly notes cross-attestation: “Mayādi (Maya, i.e., the Mayamata tradition) and others give formulas; Manasāra and Sakalādhikāra also include these methods.” citeturn50view2

Finally, *tālamāna* is treated as a *module system* anchored to the human patron/householder: the Mānuṣyālaya Candrikā commentary states that the *householder’s height is taken as one “tāla”*, and that *daṇḍa measures* (as 8-, 9-, or 10-tāla daṇḍa types) are used for proportional planning (e.g., *vīthī* widths and boundary/mandala banding). citeturn49view0turn50view2

== Text corpus, editions, and methodological notes


This report prioritizes directly citable passages from accessible witnesses of the four requested source-works, with clear flags where a detail is *not fully specified* in the cited passage.

The *Bṛhat Saṃhitā* material is taken from the *Vāstuvidyā* chapter in a bilingual pipeline: (a) an English translation stream that explicitly enumerates square numbers and deities, and (b) the Sanskrit edition view (same chapter, Sanskrit numbering) showing the base verses for grid division and deity enumeration. citeturn47view4turn48view0

The *Mānuṣyālaya Candrikā* material is drawn from an edition with Sanskrit verse citations and a running explanatory commentary (in Hindi), which is valuable here because it (i) transmits the *outer-32 and inner-deity lists as memorized ślokas*, (ii) provides *explicit Āyādi arithmetic formulas* in a compact list, and (iii) explains *marma* generation and taboo (avoid walls/columns on marma). citeturn40view0turn39view0turn50view2turn49view0

The *Mānasāra* material is cited from a full-text “Architecture of Manasara” transcription that includes (a) the description of the 81-pada plan’s line-drawing, (b) a long, stepwise deity assignment sequence for the frog-shaped plan tradition, and (c) an editorial note explicitly listing *differences* between this description and other authorities (including the Mayamata and others). It also includes the well-known outer “demoness” placements beyond the corners. citeturn15view0turn15view4turn14view1

Direct, line-citable *Māyamata / Mayamata* passages on deity-by-square placement were not reliably extractable in this session; consequently, Mayamata is integrated here through (a) explicit Mayamata citations preserved inside Mānuṣyālaya Candrikā commentary (including the famous “1 to 1024 padas” range and the list of 32 plan-names), and (b) explicit Mayamata verse-references and cross-authority comparison notes transmitted in the Mānasāra witness. This is flagged wherever it affects the completeness of “Mayamata-specific” position claims. citeturn49view0turn14view1turn15view4

== Mandala geometry and coordinate conventions used in this report


=== Classical grid definition


The *Bṛhat Saṃhitā* describes the Paramasāyika procedure: *divide the house-ground into 81 squares using 10 lines east–west and 10 lines north–south*, with *32 deities on the 32 exterior squares* and *13 “inner” placements* described next. citeturn47view4turn48view0

The Mānuṣyālaya Candrikā commentary likewise treats the Paramasāyika as *“ekāśītipada / 81-pada”*, gives proportional advice for sūtra/rajju subdivision across 81/100/64 options, and explicitly situates deity-placement and marma construction as part of padavinyāsa practice. citeturn49view0turn39view0

=== Coordinate system


To make every placement computable and comparable across sources, I use a standard *north-up grid*:

- The maṇḍala is a 9×9 lattice of equal *padas*.
- Coordinates are *(x, y)* where *x = 1..9* (west→east) and *y = 1..9* (south→north).
- Corners: SW=(1,1), SE=(9,1), NW=(1,9), NE=(9,9).

For cross-referencing the *Bṛhat Saṃhitā* square numbers, note that its translation stream supplies *square indices 1..81* in a consistent 9×9 numbering scheme (also used for the central 3×3 Brahmā block). citeturn47view4turn47view0

