== Āyādi Ṣaḍvarga formulas and worked example


=== Classical formula set as transmitted in Mānuṣyālaya Candrikā commentary


The Mānuṣyālaya Candrikā commentary gives the “Ayādi” extraction as remainder arithmetic on a perimeter-like total (“परिधि / paridhi”) and lists each divisor (and multipliers where specified), explicitly calling these “yōni, vyaya, āya, nakṣatra/ṛkṣa, tithi, vāra,” and noting cross-attestation in Mayamata (“Mayādi”), Mānasāra, and Sakalādhikāra traditions. citeturn50view2

Let *P* = the operative perimeter/measure used in the text’s “peridhi” sense (often taken as *2(L+W)* in practice; the commentary frames it as a combined measure set, “iṣṭa-ātāna-vitāna-māna-nicaya”). citeturn50view2

The formulas (as given in the excerpt) are:

- *Yoni*: multiply by *3*, divide by *8*, take remainder ⇒ yoni-index. citeturn50view2
- *Vyaya*: divide by *14* (muni-yuga), take remainder ⇒ vyaya-index. The commentary also preserves an *alternate vyaya* computation tradition (not fully expanded in the excerpt but flagged as “vyaya’s alternative side/ पक्षान्तर”). citeturn50view2
- *Āya*: multiply by *8*, divide by *12*, take remainder ⇒ āya-index. citeturn50view2
- *Nakṣatra / Ṛkṣa*: multiply by *8*, divide by *27*, take remainder ⇒ nakṣatra-index. citeturn50view2
- *Tithi*: multiply by *8*, divide by *30*, take remainder ⇒ tithi-index. citeturn50view2
- *Vāra*: divide by *7*, take remainder ⇒ weekday-index. citeturn50view2

The same passage also links yoni symbolism to directional/planetary discussions elsewhere in the commentary tradition (see matrix notes below). citeturn50view2turn37view3

=== Worked example


Assumption (explicit): the excerpted formula-set operates on *P = 2(L+W)* (a standard “perimeter” measure). The cited passage uses the term “परिधि / paridhi” and describes applying divisors to a combined dimension set; it does not, in the excerpted lines, explicitly define whether P is pure perimeter, half-perimeter, or another compound. citeturn50view2

Assume a sample plot/building rectangle:
- Length *L = 30 hasta*
- Width *W = 20 hasta*
- Then *P = 2(L+W) = 2(50) = 100 hasta*

Now compute:

==== Yoni

Compute *(3P) mod 8*
3P = 300
300 ÷ 8 = 37 remainder *4*
So *Yoni index = 4*. citeturn50view2

==== Vyaya

Compute *P mod 14*
100 ÷ 14 = 7 remainder *2*
So *Vyaya index = 2*. citeturn50view2

==== Āya

Compute *(8P) mod 12*
8P = 800
800 ÷ 12 = 66 remainder *8*
So *Āya index = 8*. citeturn50view2

==== Nakṣatra

Compute *(8P) mod 27*
8P = 800
800 ÷ 27 = 29 remainder *17*
So *Nakṣatra index = 17*. citeturn50view2

==== Tithi

Compute *(8P) mod 30*
8P = 800
800 ÷ 30 = 26 remainder *20*
So *Tithi index = 20*. citeturn50view2

==== Vāra

Compute *P mod 7*
100 ÷ 7 = 14 remainder *2*
So *Vāra index = 2*. citeturn50view2

==== Comparative logic (Āya vs Vyaya)

The Mānuṣyālaya Candrikā commentary explicitly frames *prosperity* logic as *Āya exceeding Vyaya* being wealth-producing, and the reverse being inauspicious. In our numeric example: Āya-index (8) > Vyaya-index (2), thus *“āya exceeds vyaya”* in the simplistic remainder-space sense. citeturn37view1

